**What r depends on.** The internal resistance of an electrolytic cell

- increases when the separation between the electrodes increases;
- decreases when a larger area of the electrodes is dipped in the electrolyte;
- depends on the nature and concentration of the electrolyte;
- usually decreases as the temperature of the electrolyte rises (ions move more freely);
- increases as the cell ages and is used up.

**Current and terminal voltage.** Connect a cell (ε, r) to an external resistor R. The same current I flows through R and through the cell, and the total resistance of the loop is R + r, so

$$I = \frac{\varepsilon}{R + r}$$

The terminal voltage is the potential drop across R: $V = IR = \varepsilon - Ir$. The quantity Ir is the 'lost volts', the part of the emf used to push current through the cell itself. Eliminating I gives a handy formula for the internal resistance: $r = (\varepsilon - V)/I = R(\varepsilon/V - 1)$.
